185 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Emergency Response App \024 Cardiovascular Hospital\nProduct Overview\nA mobile emergency response system used by hospital staff to receive cardiac alerts\054 understand patient context\054 and coordinate response during high\055stress scenarios\056\nThe hospital already had a working emergency app built by another vendor\056\050 The objective was not to rebuild it\054 but to simplify it for real emergency behaviour \024 especially during night shifts\056\nMy Role\nUX\057UI Designer\nWorked closely with\072\nProduct Manager\nEngineering team\nNurse heads and ward coordinators\nFocused on\072\nRestructuring alert and communication flows\nRedesigning critical emergency surfaces\nImproving information hierarchy\nAligning UI with Ionic \057 Material constraints\nIterating through prototype walkthroughs with hospital staff\nThe Real Problem\nIn cardiac emergencies\054 the first 30 seconds determine response quality\056\nIn the existing app\072\nAlerts displayed vitals\054 history\054 chat\054 and actions together\nNurses had to interpret data while deciding what action to take\nCommunication and alerts were tightly mixed\nDense layouts slowed recognition\054 especially at night\nNothing was technically broken\056\050 But cognitive load was highest at the exact moment clarity mattered most\056\nCore Design Principle\nSeparate scanning from acting\056\nUsers should first understand what\031s happening\056\050 Then decide\056\050 Then act\056\nNot all at once\056\nStructural Shift\nInstead of presenting everything simultaneously\054 the experience was divided into two clear layers\056\n1\056 Scan Layer \050Context First\051\nOpening an alert shows a single\054 large alert card containing\072\nPatient vitals \050HR\054 BP\054 O\202\051 in enlarged typography\nWard \057 room in high contrast\nAlert type and severity\nTime elapsed\nIntentionally removed from this layer\072\nCall buttons\nChat interface\nPatient history\nThis screen exists purely for fast comprehension\056\n2\056 Action Layer \050After Context\051\nOnce the situation is understood\054 users can access\072\nCall \222 Direct contact with on\055call physician\nChat \222 Team coordination\nPatient details \222 Full medical history\nActions are one tap away\054 but never competing with initial scanning\056\nThis reduced hesitation and accidental interaction during high\055stress moments\056\nInformation Architecture Redesign\nPreviously\054 alerts and communication were blended into a single experience\056\nThe system was reorganised into\072\nAlarm List\nDedicated to emergency alerts\nDesigned for speed and clarity\nCommunication Hub\nGroup chats\nMessage history\nStaff coordination\nUsers can move between chat and patient context without losing state\054 creating an interconnected experience without cluttering emergency surfaces\056\nReadability Under Pressure\nStakeholders repeatedly emphasised\072\n\034It must be readable at 2 AM\056\035\nInitial iterations increased font size slightly\056\050 This was insufficient\056\nThe adjustment\072\nIncreased overall card scale\054 not just typography\nReduced information density\nAccepted vertical expansion since nurses handle one case at a time\nImprovement came from layout scale and hierarchy \024 not decoration\056\nConstraints\nWorkflow ambiguity for 1\0232 months due to evolving AI discussions\nMid\055project stack shift from React \053 Material to Ionic\nComponent limitations requiring reuse of system elements\nInstead of redesigning the full visual system\054 effort focused on surfaces that directly affected emergency response behaviour\056\nValidation\n3 prototype iterations\n5 nurses\n2 ward coordinators\nEarly prototypes were rejected for insufficient visual scale\056\050 Later iterations improved alignment once discussions centred on speed\054 recognition\054 and error reduction\056\nOutcome\nProduct shipped and went live\nAlert flow consistently met the sub\05530\055second response target\nStaff described the experience as more comfortable than Epic\055like systems during emergencies\nReduced hesitation before first action\nThe impact was behavioural \024 not feature\055based\056\nReflection\nThis project was not about adding functionality\056\nIt was about reducing mental effort in the most critical window of use\056\nBy restructuring the system around scanning before acting\054 the app became faster\054 clearer\054 and more dependable under pressure\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 188 0 R
     /Pg 184 0 R
     /S /Span
